<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Borno Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1902 CE – 1960 CE · capital Maiduguri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Kanuri emirate under British rule that continued the ancient Kanem-Bornu monarchy from its new capital at Maiduguri.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rabih-az-zubayrs-state">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> was killed in 1900, the French and British restored the al-Kanemi dynasty, rulers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu Empire</a> since the early 19th century. In 1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abubakar-garbai">Shehu Abubakar Garbai</a> moved into British territory and was recognised as ruler of the British part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>. In 1907 he founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maiduguri">Yerwa</a>, which grew into the city of Maiduguri.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a> the shehu governed through district heads and Islamic courts, supervised by British officials of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-northern-nigeria">Protectorate of Northern Nigeria</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colonial-nigeria">Colonial Nigeria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu's</a> court titles and its tradition as a centre of Islamic scholarship. Its economy rested on farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-sanda-kyarimi">Shehu Umar Sanda Kyarimi</a> reigned from 1937 to 1968.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With Nigerian independence in 1960 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Region</a> and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borno-state">Borno State</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shehu-of-borno">Shehu of Borno</a> remains one of the most senior traditional rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1902 CE – 1960 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:97.55%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1902 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maiduguri</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abubakar Garbai, Umar Sanda Kyarimi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kanuri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bornu Emirate, Emirate of Borno</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1931&amp;focus=borno-emirate" aria-label="Borno Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5042 1736 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Borno Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5362 1992L5399 1946M5362 1992L5170 2068M5362 1992L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5399 1946h0M5170 2068h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 1992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 1992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1931&amp;focus=borno-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
